  #sol-item(1, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The crossing of a national frontier is what makes it foreign.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Only goods are visible; the balance of trade counts goods alone.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The current account adds services and transfers to the visible balance.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A good you can see crossing the frontier is a visible export.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    They are services, so they are invisible.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    950 − 800 = 150, and imports are larger, so it is a deficit.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    −150 + 220 = +70, a current account surplus.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Double-entry bookkeeping means the accounts must balance even when trade does not.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The index compares what you sell with what you buy.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    120 ÷ 150 × 100 = 80: each unit of exports buys less than before.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The country must sell more to buy the same imports.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    It raises the price of the imported good and protects the local producer.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Quotas restrict quantity; tariffs raise price.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Complete prohibition is the strongest of the restrictions.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Permission is granted up to a stated quantity or value.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Without foreign exchange the importer cannot pay, so the import dies.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The subsidy lets a firm sell below the import price without making a loss.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A quota limits quantity without raising revenue for the state.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    It states the goods, quantity, quality and terms the importer wants.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    It is a quotation in invoice form, used to obtain a licence or a letter of credit.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    All three functions at once is why the banks insist on it.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Origin matters for tariff rates and for quota allocation.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The drawer orders the drawee to pay the payee.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The bank promises to pay against the documents, which removes the exporter's risk.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The consul checks the declared value and origin.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The goods enter one country and leave again, often after processing or repacking.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    A service bought from abroad is an invisible import.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The capital account and reserves are what close the gap.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Foreign buyers need fewer of their units to buy the same export.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The improvement is measured by what a unit of export will now buy.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The commercial invoice is the seller's claim for payment.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Only the carrier can acknowledge that the goods are on board.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Title passes by delivery of the document, which is what makes it valuable to a bank.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Infant industries need protection until they reach efficient scale.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    The tariff is paid by the consumer, not by the foreigner.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Two parties, two countries, one agreement.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Most-favoured-nation treatment is the classic multilateral principle.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Receipts exceed payments, so the country acquires claims on foreigners.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
    Only the tariff is a tax.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 4.38pt)[
